rep(", and Section 10 gives a reading order.\"));",
    ", Section 10 gives a reading order, and Section 11 identifies which of the open questions can be addressed within the EPCAPE turbulence–brightness project.\"));")

# ---- helper: paragraph with bold lead-in ----
rep("const Num = (t, ref = \"nums\") =>",
    "const PB = (lead, t) => { const parts = t.split(\"*\"); const runs = [new TextRun({ text: lead, bold: true, font: FONT, size: BODY })].concat(parts.map((s, i) => new TextRun({ text: s, italics: i % 2 === 1, font: FONT, size: BODY }))); return new Paragraph({ children: runs, spacing: { after: 160, line: 276 }, alignment: AlignmentType.JUSTIFIED }); };\nconst Num = (t, ref = \"nums\") =>")

section11 = r'''// 11. Project-specific open questions
children.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new PageBreak()] }));
children.push(H("11. Open questions addressable within the EPCAPE turbulence–brightness project", HeadingLevel.HEADING_1));
children.push(P("This section asks which of the open questions in Section 9 can be answered within the project *Connecting Aerosol-Related Cloud Brightness to Turbulence using EPCAPE Measurements* (Miller et al., 2024), given that the aerosol side of that project has been largely completed by Dedrick et al. (2026) and that the turbulence side is assigned to the principal investigator. It is written as a planning aid, not as a literature synthesis: the judgments about scope and overlap are this review's own reading of the proposal and are stated with the passages they rest on so that they can be checked."));

children.push(H("11.1 The proposal and how its work is divided", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("The proposal, submitted to the DOE Atmospheric System Research program under FOA DE-FOA-0002850 (topic 3, warm boundary-layer processes), is led by Mark Miller (Rutgers) with Lynn Russell and Dan Lubin (Scripps) as co-investigators. Its central argument is that marine boundary-layer cloud brightness depends on the nature of the aerosol and its history of in-cloud processing through the particles' critical supersaturations (S∗), and on local in-cloud turbulence through the ambient supersaturation (S), so that the distributions of S and S∗ and their history are essential to understanding brightening (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 1). It states three hypotheses: (H1) aerosols control cloud supersaturation in some conditions, typically low particle concentrations and low updraft velocities, by taking up water more quickly and reducing the maximum supersaturation; (H2) turbulence-driven updraft velocities increase supersaturation, which increases brightness; and (H3) cloud-top radiative cooling increases cloud brightness by increasing in-cloud updraft velocities and therefore supersaturation (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 3). A first objective asks whether retrieved cloud properties reflect the regional signatures of aerosol (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 3). Every hypothesis ends in cloud brightness."));
children.push(P("The proposal assigns the work explicitly. The principal investigator will synthesize the sensors at Scripps Pier and Mt. Soledad, assist with retrieval of S and S_max, calculate S from retrieved turbulence and thermodynamic parameters using the quasi-steady-state, Abdul-Razzak–Ghan and remote-sensing-based formulations given as its equations 3–5, characterize boundary-layer turbulence and decoupling, and coordinate the project. Co-I Russell will analyze in situ cloud and aerosol size distributions to characterize S∗ and retrieve a Hoppel-minimum-based closure on S. Co-I Lubin “will retrieve in-cloud microphysical quantities and perform radiation transfer calculations to relate cloud brightness to measurements of in-cloud turbulence and differences in S and S_max, which will be used, in part, as a surrogate of the impacts of turbulence on the CDSS” (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 1). The timeline gives Lubin radiative transfer calculations of shortwave cloud brightness in year 1 and their analysis in the context of boundary-layer turbulence, with a publication, in year 2 (Miller et al., 2024, timeline table). The radiation plan (Sec. 2.3) names the instruments: the MFRSR, AERI, SKYRAD and microwave radiometer at the pier; the CC-RIDER suite at Mt. Soledad (broadband pyranometers, a near-infrared pyranometer covering 780–2800 nm, a total-ultraviolet radiometer covering 295–385 nm, and a pyrgeometer) used with the Mt. Soledad microwave radiometer to retrieve r_e following Dong et al. (1997, 1998) and conservative-scattering optical depth from the ultraviolet channel; and MODIS/VIIRS, CERES Cloud Radiative Swath Edition 4 and GOES-West SatCORPS retrievals for the top-of-atmosphere view."));
children.push(P("Dedrick et al. (2026) completed most of the Russell component. Using one year of EPCAPE data, they classified aerosol size distributions into broad-accumulation (38–39% of the time), bimodal (34–37%) and broad-Aitken (25–27%) clusters, associated the broad clusters with recent regional emissions and the bimodal cluster with repeated cloud cycling, quantified upwind and on-site scavenging (an e-folding time of about 2.5 h for in-cloud Aitken-mode loss at Mt. Soledad), and compared four supersaturation retrievals. Cloud-scene and parcel retrievals gave 0.03%–0.13% and Hoppel-minimum retrievals 0.2%–0.3%, the latter interpreted as maxima over ensembles of recent cloud cycles; the cloud-scene method was judged the most reliable; and retrieved supersaturations correlated positively with liquid water content and droplet number (Dedrick et al., 2026). They concluded that at EPCAPE the signatures of local sources and scavenging overwhelm the competition signature identified in remote environments, and that efficient scavenging in polluted coastal air “limits the cloud impacts of adding particles” (Dedrick et al., 2026)."));
children.push(P("Three features of that paper bear directly on what remains. First, it reports no radiative quantity: optical depth, albedo and surface or top-of-atmosphere irradiance do not appear, so the statement about limited cloud impacts is an inference from microphysics that has not been tested radiatively. Second, the cloud-scene and Hoppel-minimum supersaturations showed only weak positive correlations with the free convective velocity w∗, which the authors read as no clear link between supersaturation and w∗ for these coastal clouds (Dedrick et al., 2026); this weakens, for these methods, the first link of H2. Third, its preferred supersaturation retrieval for the low-cloud periods uses the ARM NDROP droplet number, which requires MFRSR optical depth and is therefore available only in daytime (Dedrick et al., 2026). Of the five supersaturation methods listed in the proposal, four are reported; the quasi-steady-state estimate is not among them, and the authors note that additional retrievals were evaluated but not reported because of data or other limitations (Dedrick et al., 2026, Text S10). Table 3 summarizes the status of each component."));

const t3c1 = 2900, t3c2 = 1500, t3c3 = 2900, t3c4 = 2060;
const rows3 = [
  ["Component (proposal section)", "Assigned to", "Status, November 2026", "Basis"],
  ["S∗ distributions and Hoppel-minimum closure on S (Secs. 1, 2.4)", "Russell", "Addressed.", "Dedrick et al. 2026"],
  ["Comparison of supersaturation retrieval methods (Sec. 2.5; year 2)", "Miller, Russell", "Largely addressed: four of five methods compared; quasi-steady-state estimate not reported.", "Dedrick et al. 2026"],
  ["Supersaturation from turbulence via the proposal's eqs. 3–5; cloud-base updraft distributions; turbulence profiles and decoupling (Secs. 2.3, 2.5)", "Miller", "In progress in the PI's group (unpublished work presented at the August 2026 EPCAPE science meeting); no published EPCAPE analysis found.", "Miller et al. 2024"],
  ["Radiometric retrieval of r_e, τ and N_d from CC-RIDER, TUVR, MFRSR, AERI and MWR (Sec. 2.3)", "Lubin", "Not done beyond the standard ARM products; Dedrick et al. used NDROP.", "Miller et al. 2024; Dedrick et al. 2026"],
  ["Radiative transfer calculation of shortwave cloud brightness and its relation to turbulence and S (Sec. 1; timeline)", "Lubin", "Open.", "Miller et al. 2024"],
  ["Cloud-top net shortwave and longwave radiation computed from retrievals (proposal Table 2), the input to H3", "Lubin (computed); used by Miller", "Open.", "Miller et al. 2024"],
  ["Satellite top-of-atmosphere analysis: MODIS/VIIRS, CERES CRS, GOES SatCORPS (Sec. 2.3)", "Lubin", "Open.", "Miller et al. 2024"],
  ["Objective 1: do retrieved cloud properties reflect regional aerosol signatures? (Sec. 3)", "Whole team", "Partly: aerosol clusters linked to air-mass source and cloudiness, and LWP linked to CCN; not yet linked to retrieved optical properties or brightness.", "Dedrick et al. 2026; Zhang et al. 2025"],
  ["Mesoscale organization and its dependence on aerosol loading (Sec. 3)", "Not assigned", "Open.", "Miller et al. 2024"],
];
const tableRows3 = rows3.map((r, i) => new TableRow({
  tableHeader: i === 0, cantSplit: true,
  children: [cell(r[0], t3c1, i === 0, i === 0 ? "D9E2F3" : null), cell(r[1], t3c2, i === 0, i === 0 ? "D9E2F3" : null), cell(r[2], t3c3, i === 0, i === 0 ? "D9E2F3" : null), cell(r[3], t3c4, i === 0, i === 0 ? "D9E2F3" : null)]
}));
children.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new TextRun({ text: "Table 3. Status of the components of Miller et al. (2024) as of November 2026.", bold: true, font: FONT, size: 20 })], spacing: { before: 120, after: 80 } }));
children.push(new Table({ columnWidths: [t3c1, t3c2, t3c3, t3c4], width: { size: tw, type: WidthType.DXA }, rows: tableRows3 }));
children.push(new Paragraph({ spacing: { after: 200 } }));

children.push(H("11.2 Selection criteria", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("A question is listed below only if it meets five conditions: (1) it is open in the literature reviewed in Sections 8–9; (2) it falls within a task the proposal assigns to Co-I Lubin or leaves unassigned; (3) it uses the principal investigator's turbulence, updraft and coupling products only as inputs, for stratification or as covariates, rather than producing them; (4) it does not repeat Dedrick et al. (2026) or the published EPCAPE analysis of Zhang et al. (2025); and (5) it can be attempted with archived EPCAPE data. The candidates are ordered by how directly they answer the question the proposal leaves open after Dedrick et al. (2026), namely whether the aerosol and supersaturation differences that paper documents change cloud brightness, and then by feasibility."));

children.push(H("11.3 Candidate questions", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(PB("A. Does the brightness response to aerosol at EPCAPE show a diminished Twomey effect, and does it differ between aerosol regimes? ", "This asks for the albedo and optical-depth susceptibilities (dα_c/dln N_acc and dln τ/dln N_acc at fixed LWP) and the droplet-activation sensitivity (dln N_d/dln N_acc), stratified by the three size-distribution clusters of Dedrick et al. (2026) and by coupling state, followed where the data allow by the activation–competition decomposition that Dedrick et al. (2025) applied at LASIC. It addresses questions 5 and 11 of Section 9. At LASIC the competition response reduced the activation response to the Twomey effect by 12% using a parcel-based supersaturation and by up to 35% using the scene-based supersaturation, and it grew from clean to smoky conditions (Dedrick et al., 2025). Dedrick et al. (2026) inferred a different control at EPCAPE (scavenging rather than competition) but did not measure the radiative outcome. A theoretical expectation is available: the retrieved EPCAPE supersaturations of about 0.1% lie below the S_max of about 0.2% that Reutter et al. (2009) associate with the updraft-limited regime, in which N_d becomes insensitive to aerosol number; this placement is this review's inference, not a result of either paper. The testable prediction is therefore a smaller dln N_d/dln N_acc, and a smaller albedo susceptibility, in the polluted broad-mode clusters than in the bimodal cluster. A finding of no difference would also be informative, because it would contradict the scavenging-limits-impact inference. The question sits squarely in the proposal's first objective and H1, uses Lubin's retrievals and radiative transfer, and needs from the principal investigator's group only the coupling classification and, for the dynamical term of the decomposition, measured cloud-base updrafts, a term best computed jointly with that group. Data: the size-distribution and CCN data archived by the Russell group at the UC San Diego Library (see the data availability statement of Dedrick et al., 2026), MWR LWP, MFRSR optical depth and r_e, NDROP N_d, FM-120 droplet spectra at Mt. Soledad, SKYRAD and CC-RIDER irradiances, and libRadtran (Emde et al., 2016) to convert optical properties to albedo. The main risk is circularity: NDROP derives N_d from MFRSR optical depth and MWR LWP, and the cloud-scene supersaturation that Dedrick et al. (2026) prefer is built on NDROP, so regressing optical depth on that supersaturation is partly circular. Using aerosol number or CCN as the independent variable and the in situ FM-120 number as an independent check reduces this; Candidate B addresses it directly. A second risk is sample size: the proposal cites more than 700 hours with Mt. Soledad in cloud, but after the filters of Dedrick et al. (2026) there were 24 cloud events totalling 214 h and 374 h of low-cloud periods, before restriction to daytime, overcast, single-layer, coupled and non-drizzling scenes. Dedrick et al. (2025) also note that ACI_N carries uncertainty of up to 78% from the approximations used to derive N_d."));
children.push(PB("B. Which EPCAPE microphysical retrievals are independent and accurate enough for ACI analysis? ", "This asks for an error-characterized retrieval of optical depth, r_e and N_d from the CC-RIDER broadband and near-infrared pyranometers, the ultraviolet radiometer and the Mt. Soledad microwave radiometer, as the proposal plans (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 2.3; Dong et al., 1997, 1998), compared with the MFRSR, NDROP and lidar-based products and with the FM-120 droplet spectra measured in cloud at the same site. It addresses questions 7 and 18 of Section 9. The need is documented: an evaluation of four ground-based N_d retrievals against aircraft data at the Eastern North Atlantic site found that NDROP overestimates N_d for broken clouds or clouds with low LWP while agreeing for overcast clouds with LWP above about 25 g m⁻², that a radar-based retrieval was consistently low with a median error of about 35%, and that a micropulse-lidar method agreed best and works day and night (Zhang et al., 2023); NDROP was earlier found to produce distributions that are too broad at low LWP (Lim et al., 2016). EPCAPE operated a micropulse lidar (Russell et al., 2025), so a lidar-based N_d could also extend the cloud-scene supersaturation retrieval of Dedrick et al. (2026) to night. What EPCAPE adds that ENA lacked is a long record of in-cloud droplet spectra collocated with surface radiometers. The question is entirely within Lubin's task and does not touch the turbulence work. Its caveats are physical: when Mt. Soledad is in cloud its radiometers see only the part of the cloud above the site, and the FM-120 samples near the bottom of that column, whereas solar retrievals weight the upper cloud (Dong et al., 1998), so the comparison requires an explicit vertical profile model; and the proposal states that the ultraviolet radiometer data still required post-season calibration and algorithm validation at the time of writing (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 2.3), which should be confirmed with Co-I Lubin. The SCARPE campaign description also notes that without an independent LWP the optical depth is much less sensitive to r_e (ARM, EPCAPE-SCARPE campaign page), which is why the microwave radiometer at Mt. Soledad is essential."));
children.push(PB("C. How much does droplet-spectrum width offset the Twomey brightening at EPCAPE? ", "This asks how the relative dispersion of the droplet spectrum (σ_r/r̄) measured by the FM-120 varies with aerosol cluster, N_d and retrieved supersaturation, and how much the resulting change in spectral shape offsets or adds to the albedo change computed in Candidate A. Liu and Daum (2002) showed that aerosol-induced changes in spectral shape act to diminish the cooling of the Twomey effect, and the proposal notes that the albedo response can change with dispersion depending on the mechanism that produces it and that observed dispersion often exceeds what models produce (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 1, citing Feingold et al., 1997; Liu and Daum, 2002; Feingold and Siebert, 2009). The proposal lists the droplet number, mean radius and spectral width measured in cloud at Mt. Soledad among its variables (Miller et al., 2024, Table 2) and frames Lubin's supersaturation-based analysis as a surrogate for turbulence effects on the droplet spectrum. The question is therefore in scope, but it has the largest overlap risk of the five: the mechanism of turbulent broadening (supersaturation fluctuations, eddy hopping) belongs to the turbulence side of the project, so the defensible division is that this candidate quantifies the aerosol dependence and the radiative consequence of the measured width, using turbulence measures only as covariates. Caveats: the FM-120 covers 2–50 μm optical diameter (Dedrick et al., 2026), sampling is at one level near cloud base, and microphysical relationships in stratocumulus vary with height (Yeom et al., 2021), so a single-level dispersion may not represent the column that sets the albedo."));
children.push(PB("D. Is an aerosol signal detectable in surface shortwave transmission under coastal stratocumulus? ", "This is question 8 of Section 9, restated for the project. The only long-term surface study found no daily aerosol signal at the Southern Great Plains, where LWP explained most of the variance (Sena et al., 2016). The design that makes either outcome publishable is to compute the expected transmission change per unit change in ln N_acc at fixed LWP and solar zenith angle with libRadtran before examining the data, then test overcast, single-layer, coupled, non-drizzling scenes from SKYRAD at the pier and CC-RIDER at Mt. Soledad against that expectation. The proposal's own argument that the near-infrared pyranometer is more sensitive to r_e than a broadband instrument, because liquid water absorbs in that band (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 2.3), offers a second aerosol-sensitive quantity, the near-infrared-to-broadband ratio at fixed LWP, that does not depend on any retrieval product. The candidate is within Lubin's radiometric task and does not involve the turbulence work. It can stand alone or be folded into Candidate A as a retrieval-independent radiative metric. The risk is that the signal is below the noise set by LWP variability; Section 7 notes that the transmission-susceptibility expression used there to estimate its size is this review's own extrapolation and must be replaced by explicit radiative transfer."));
children.push(PB("E. Is the EPCAPE brightness response visible at the top of the atmosphere, and does it depend on mesoscale morphology? ", "This asks for top-of-atmosphere albedo computed from the surface-retrieved cloud properties and compared with CERES and GOES SatCORPS retrievals for matched scenes, and for the susceptibilities from Candidate A recomputed from the satellite view and stratified by cloud morphology. It addresses questions 1, 11 and 17 of Section 9. ACI metrics depend on averaging scale (McComiskey and Feingold, 2012), and susceptibilities vary within stratocumulus regimes with cloud morphology (Goren et al., 2026), so whether a point-based surface susceptibility reproduces the satellite one is an open and testable question. It is in scope: the proposal states that “if turbulent effects are robust, we should expect to detect them in CERES TOA observations” and plans to examine mesoscale organization and its dependence on aerosol loading (Miller et al., 2024, Secs. 2.3 and 3). It also produces a time-resolved brightness series that the principal investigator's group can use to test H2, which is a contribution to, not a duplication of, that work. Caveats: the 20-km CERES footprint mixes land and ocean at this coastal site, GOES retrievals are half-hourly, and the proposal shows that orography modifies turbulence at Mt. Soledad on some days (Miller et al., 2024, Fig. 1)."));

children.push(H("11.4 The boundary with the turbulence work", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("H2 and H3 both end in brightness, and brightness comes only from the radiation side of the project, so the boundary is best drawn by product rather than by hypothesis. On the principal investigator's side are turbulence profiles from Doppler lidar and radar, decoupling diagnostics, cloud-base updraft distributions and supersaturation computed from them with the proposal's equations 3–5; the PI has previously published on the joint turbulence and radiation structure of stratocumulus-topped boundary layers (Ghate et al., 2014), which supports treating the turbulence half of H3 as his. On the radiation side are calibrated optical depth, albedo and irradiance time series at the surface, at cloud level and at the top of the atmosphere, and the cloud-top shortwave heating and longwave cooling computed from retrieved cloud properties and soundings, which the proposal lists as a computed variable (Miller et al., 2024, Table 2) and which H3 requires as input. Two observations suggest that the joint test of H2 should allow for a path through LWP rather than only through supersaturation: Dedrick et al. (2026) found no clear link between supersaturation and w∗ for these clouds, and the proposal's preliminary ultraviolet-radiometer result suggested that stronger sub-cloud turbulence was associated with larger optical depth after sunrise and smaller optical depth in the early afternoon, with the caution that those data were not yet calibrated (Miller et al., 2024, Fig. 5)."));
children.push(P("Four lines of work are not recommended because they duplicate completed or assigned work or fall outside the proposal: re-deriving supersaturation from Hoppel minima or CCN closure (Dedrick et al., 2026); deriving turbulence statistics, decoupling diagnostics or turbulence-based supersaturation (assigned to the principal investigator); repeating the machine-learning LWP-adjustment analysis of Zhang et al. (2025) without a new angle; and drizzle or precipitation processes, which the proposal's case-selection criteria exclude (Miller et al., 2024, Sec. 2.1)."));

children.push(H("11.5 Summary and recommended sequence", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
const t4c1 = 1900, t4c2 = 1900, t4c3 = 1900, t4c4 = 1660, t4c5 = 2000;
const rows4 = [
  ["Candidate", "Open question (Section 9)", "Hook in the proposal", "Overlap with PI", "Main risk"],
  ["A. Brightness response by aerosol regime", "Q5, Q11", "Objective 1; H1; Lubin retrievals and radiative transfer", "Low: uses coupling flags and updrafts as inputs", "Circularity among ARM products; sample size"],
  ["B. Independent, validated microphysics retrieval", "Q7, Q18", "Lubin's CC-RIDER, ultraviolet radiometer and MWR plan (Sec. 2.3)", "None", "Partial-column geometry; radiometer calibration"],
  ["C. Dispersion offset to the Twomey effect", "Dispersion effect (Sec. 11.3)", "Proposal introduction; Lubin's supersaturation surrogate for the droplet spectrum", "Moderate: the broadening mechanism is turbulence work", "Single-level, limited-range droplet spectra"],
  ["D. Surface transmission signal", "Q8", "SKYRAD and CC-RIDER; Lubin radiative transfer", "None", "Signal below LWP-driven noise"],
  ["E. Surface-to-TOA closure and morphology", "Q1, Q11, Q17", "Satellite plan; mesoscale-organization focus", "Low: supplies brightness for H2", "Footprint mismatch at a coastal site"],
];
const tableRows4 = rows4.map((r, i) => new TableRow({
  tableHeader: i === 0, cantSplit: true,
  children: [cell(r[0], t4c1, i === 0, i === 0 ? "D9E2F3" : null), cell(r[1], t4c2, i === 0, i === 0 ? "D9E2F3" : null), cell(r[2], t4c3, i === 0, i === 0 ? "D9E2F3" : null), cell(r[3], t4c4, i === 0, i === 0 ? "D9E2F3" : null), cell(r[4], t4c5, i === 0, i === 0 ? "D9E2F3" : null)]
}));
children.push(new Paragraph({ children: [new TextRun({ text: "Table 4. Candidate questions within the radiation and retrieval tasks of Miller et al. (2024).", bold: true, font: FONT, size: 20 })], spacing: { before: 120, after: 80 } }));
children.push(new Table({ columnWidths: [t4c1, t4c2, t4c3, t4c4, t4c5], width: { size: tw, type: WidthType.DXA }, rows: tableRows4 }));
children.push(new Paragraph({ spacing: { after: 200 } }));
children.push(P("A defensible sequence, offered as a starting point for discussion with the co-investigators rather than as a settled plan, is to begin with Candidate A, because it answers the question the proposal leaves open after Dedrick et al. (2026) and can start from archived ARM products; to run the validation part of Candidate B in parallel, because circularity among those products is the main threat to Candidate A's result; to fold Candidate D into Candidate A as a retrieval-independent radiative metric; and to treat Candidates C and E as follow-on studies. Given the low supersaturations and high aerosol concentrations at EPCAPE, the most likely outcome of Candidate A is a weak Twomey response in the polluted clusters. That outcome is publishable as a quantified, radiatively measured contrast with the remote-marine LASIC result (Dedrick et al., 2025), but the analysis should be designed from the start so that a weak or null response can be distinguished from a retrieval artifact."));

'''
rep("// References\n", section11 + "// References\n")

# ---- references ----
new_refs = [
"Ghate, V. P., Albrecht, B. A., Miller, M. A., Brewer, A., & Fairall, C. W. (2014). Turbulence and radiation in stratocumulus-topped marine boundary layers: A case study from VOCALS-REx. Journal of Applied Meteorology and Climatology, 53, 117–135. https://doi.org/10.1175/JAMC-D-12-0225.1 [folder]",
"Liu, Y., & Daum, P. H. (2002). Indirect warming effect from dispersion forcing. Nature, 419, 580–581. https://doi.org/10.1038/419580a",
"Miller, M. A., Russell, L. M., & Lubin, D. (2024). Connecting aerosol-related cloud brightness to turbulence using EPCAPE measurements. Proposal to the U.S. Department of Energy Atmospheric System Research program, FOA DE-FOA-0002850 (version dated 10 February 2024). [folder]",
"Zhang, D., Vogelmann, A. M., Yang, F., Luke, E., Kollias, P., Wang, Z., Wu, P., Gustafson, W. I., Jr., Mei, F., Glienke, S., Tomlinson, J., & Desai, N. (2023). Evaluation of four ground-based retrievals of cloud droplet number concentration in marine stratocumulus with aircraft in situ measurements. Atmospheric Measurement Techniques, 16, 5827–5846. https://doi.org/10.5194/amt-16-5827-2023",
]
start = src.index("const refs = [\n") + len("const refs = [\n")
end = src.index("\n];\nrefs.forEach")
entries = [l for l in src[start:end].split("\n") if l.strip()]
def key(e):
    s = e.strip().strip('",').replace("&", "").replace("Jr.,", "")
    return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9 ]", "", s.lower())
for nr in new_refs:
    line = '"' + nr.replace('"', '\\"') + '",'
    k = key(line)
    pos = next((i for i, e in enumerate(entries) if key(e) > k), len(entries))
    entries.insert(pos, line)
src = src[:start] + "\n".join(entries) + src[end:]

rep('fs.writeFileSync("ACI_Radiative_Impacts_Literature_Review_v2.docx", buf)',
    'fs.writeFileSync("ACI_Radiative_Impacts_Literature_Review_v3.docx", buf)')

open(p_out, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(src)
print("ok; refs:", len(entries))
